igns, including those involving AJ, relies on several assumptions:\n1. The effects of factors are additive.\n2. The errors are normally distributed and independent.\n3. The variance of errors is constant across all levels of the factors (homoscedasticity).\n4. The factors are fixed and not random.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nFractional factorial designs, and by extension AJ, are particularly useful when:\n- The number of factors is large, making a full factorial design impractical.\n- The researcher is interested in screening factors to identify significant ones.\n- Resources (time, budget, materials) are limited.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nThe analysis of data from fractional factorial designs typically involves:\n1. Estimating the effects of factors using regression analysis.\n2. Conducting ANOVA to determine the significance of the effects.\n3. Performing diagnostics to check the assumptions of the model.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\n- Efficient use of resources by reducing the number of experimental runs.\n- Ability to identify significant factors and interactions quickly.\n- Flexibility in design allows for the exploration of multiple factors simultaneously.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\n- Potential for confounding effects, where the effects of two or more factors cannot be separated.\n- Limited ability to estimate higher-order interactions unless specifically designed to do so.\n- Requires careful selection of factors to avoid missing critical interactions.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\nAJ is related to other designs such as full factorial designs, Plackett-Burman designs, and response surface methodology. Each of these has its own strengths and weaknesses depending on the experimental context.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations:**\nWhen implementing fractional factorial designs, researchers should consider:\n- The selection of factors and levels to ensure meaningful results.\n- The po